{#
    Puts the search page's filter panel in place without the theme being touched.

    OXID 7 resolves this by directory convention: a file under
    views/twig/extensions/themes/<theme>/<the shop template's own path> joins
    that template's inheritance chain.

    Deliberately under `apex` rather than under `default`, which would apply to
    every theme. Both halves of this are apex's: the block names it happens to
    use, and the markup and stylesheet that were shaped against it. A different
    theme naming its blocks differently would get nothing, and one that named
    them the same would get markup built for someone else's layout - the second
    being the worse of the two, because it looks like it worked.

    So on another theme this file is a **worked example**: copy the directory to
    your own theme's id, point the blocks at the ones your theme defines, and
    keep or replace the partials it includes.

    Every block calls parent() first, so the theme renders exactly what it
    rendered before and the module's markup follows.
#}
{% extends "page/search/search.html.twig" %}

{% block search_header %}
    {{ parent() }}

    {# Only the correction line here - it explains the result as a whole and
       belongs with the headline. The guard keeps an empty container out of the
       markup on the ordinary case, which is every search spelled correctly. #}
    {% if oView.getFoun10SearchCorrection() %}
        <div class="container-xxl">
            {% include "@foun10EasySearch/frontend/correction.html.twig" %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block search_results %}
    {#
        The chips, for the case where the filter matched nothing.

        The theme renders its whole locator row only when there are results, so
        on an empty result there is no row to sit below - and that is precisely
        when a customer needs to see and undo what they picked, rather than be
        left on an empty page with only the browser's back button.

        Guarded on getArticleList() rather than on getArticleCount(), because
        that is the condition the theme itself puts around the product block
        below. Using the count instead renders the chips twice on an empty
        result: the list is an object and stays truthy even with nothing in it.
    #}
    {% if not oView.getArticleList() %}
        <div class="container-xxl">
            {% include "@foun10EasySearch/frontend/active-filters.html.twig" %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {{ parent() }}

    {# An offcanvas, so it can sit at the end of the page regardless of where
       the button that opens it ended up. #}
    {% include "@foun10EasySearch/frontend/panel.html.twig" %}
{% endblock %}

{% block search_list_productlist %}
    {#
        The chips in the normal case: directly below the locator row and above
        the products, spanning the full width.

        A col-12 rather than a bare div because this block's content are the
        children of the theme's grid row - anything else there would be laid out
        as if it were a product column.
    #}
    <div class="col-12">
        {% include "@foun10EasySearch/frontend/active-filters.html.twig" %}
    </div>

    {{ parent() }}
{% endblock %}
